From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: h265: never leave a DPB slot stale

cedrus_h265_frame_info_write_dpb() wrote only the active DPB entries
whose reference buffer it could find, and skipped the rest. A skipped
slot keeps the addresses an earlier job, or an earlier process, put
there. The reference lists can name such a slot: a reference the client
no longer holds, or an index past num_active_dpb_entries, both of which
a damaged stream produces, especially when the client keeps decoding
after a failed frame.

On the H713 the bit-flipped HEVC stream b03-h, decoded through
GStreamer va, logged 2-11 missing references per run, each one a slot
the VE could have followed into a buffer freed long ago.

This is hardening, not the cause of that stream's IOMMU page faults.
Those continued with this patch at the same rate: logging every address
cedrus programs showed all buffers, DPB slots and reference-list indices
valid, while the faulting addresses were scattered across the whole
32-bit space. They track damaged slice payload, so the VE most likely
computes reference fetches from corrupt motion vectors without clamping
them; kernel 0158 is what makes the VE recover from them.

Write all 16 slots on every job. A slot with no reference behind it
gets the picture being decoded, as hantro does: its prediction is
garbage, which a damaged stream's is anyway, but every address the
engine can follow is mapped. Missing references are reported,
ratelimited.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/a4d6d5f38832ddee2e4619607bf3ba925dd7012d/patches/kernel/0159-media-cedrus-h265-never-leave-a-dpb-slot-stale.patch (0159-media-cedrus-h265-never-leave-a-dpb-slot-stale.patch, commit aae688d)
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_h265.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_h265.c
@@ -139,28 +139,53 @@
 	cedrus_h265_sram_write_data(dev, &frame_info, sizeof(frame_info));
 }
 
+/*
+ * Write every DPB slot the reference lists can index, not only the active
+ * ones with a buffer behind them. A slot left alone keeps the addresses of
+ * whatever an earlier job (or an earlier process) put there, and the
+ * reference lists of a damaged stream - or of the frames a client keeps
+ * decoding after one failed - name missing references and inactive slots,
+ * which would send the VE into buffers freed long ago. A slot
+ * with no reference gets the picture being decoded, as hantro does: the
+ * prediction is garbage, which a damaged stream is anyway, but every address
+ * the engine can follow is mapped.
+ */
 static void cedrus_h265_frame_info_write_dpb(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx,
 					     const struct v4l2_hevc_dpb_entry *dpb,
-					     u8 num_active_dpb_entries)
+					     u8 num_active_dpb_entries,
+					     struct vb2_buffer *fallback)
 {
 	struct vb2_queue *vq = v4l2_m2m_get_vq(ctx->fh.m2m_ctx,
 					       V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE);
-	unsigned int i;
+	unsigned int i, missing = 0;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < V4L2_HEVC_DPB_ENTRIES_NUM_MAX; i++) {
+		struct vb2_buffer *buf = NULL;
+		u32 pic_order_cnt[2] = { 0, 0 };
+		bool field_pic = false;
+
+		if (i < num_active_dpb_entries) {
+			buf = vb2_find_buffer(vq, dpb[i].timestamp);
+			pic_order_cnt[0] = dpb[i].pic_order_cnt_val;
+			pic_order_cnt[1] = dpb[i].pic_order_cnt_val;
+			field_pic = dpb[i].field_pic;
+			if (!buf)
+				missing++;
+		}
 
-	for (i = 0; i < num_active_dpb_entries; i++) {
-		struct vb2_buffer *buf = vb2_find_buffer(vq, dpb[i].timestamp);
-		u32 pic_order_cnt[2] = {
-			dpb[i].pic_order_cnt_val,
-			dpb[i].pic_order_cnt_val
-		};
-
-		if (!buf)
-			continue;
-
-		cedrus_h265_frame_info_write_single(ctx, i, dpb[i].field_pic,
-						    pic_order_cnt,
-						    buf);
+		if (!buf) {
+			buf = fallback;
+			field_pic = false;
+		}
+
+		cedrus_h265_frame_info_write_single(ctx, i, field_pic,
+						    pic_order_cnt, buf);
 	}
+
+	if (missing)
+		dev_warn_ratelimited(ctx->dev->dev,
+				     "h265: %u of %u references not found, using the output picture\n",
+				     missing, num_active_dpb_entries);
 }
 
 static void cedrus_h265_ref_pic_list_write(struct cedrus_dev *dev,
@@ -755,7 +781,8 @@
 
 	/* Write decoded picture buffer in pic list. */
 	cedrus_h265_frame_info_write_dpb(ctx, decode_params->dpb,
-					 decode_params->num_active_dpb_entries);
+					 decode_params->num_active_dpb_entries,
+					 &run->dst->vb2_buf);
 
 	/* Output frame. */
 
